$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A marquee project, a familiar question</h2>
<p>The contracts behind this petition are among the largest construction engagements in the National Capital Region: EPC packages of roughly Rs 1,892 crore and Rs 2,690 crore for the first phase of the Noida International Airport. When the relationship soured — claims of about Rs 1,113 crore on one side, counterclaims of about Rs 437 crore on the other, a Rs 500 crore settlement agreement in February 2026, and then encashments of about Rs 329 crore in guarantees — the contractor came to court under Section 9 seeking its money back and a freeze on the remaining retention guarantees.</p>
<p>The legal question, however, was the oldest one in bank guarantee litigation: when may a court stop, or undo, the encashment of an unconditional guarantee?</p>

<h2>The twin gateway, applied</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>What the contractor argued</strong><br>Special equities: the employer&rsquo;s own conduct, including additional works and the timing of the invocations after the settlement, made the encashments inequitable and warranted restitution and protection of the unencashed guarantees.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>What the Court held</strong><br>No fraud was alleged at all. The encashments raise purely monetary issues, fully capable of restitution in arbitration if the invocations are found wrongful. Compensable monetary loss is not irretrievable injustice, and the merits of the invocations are for the arbitral tribunal, not a Section 9 court.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court followed the Division Bench&rsquo;s recent restatement in <em>Vishal Infrastructure</em> and the Supreme Court&rsquo;s consistent line that an unconditional bank guarantee is an independent commitment the beneficiary may call upon, with judicial interference confined to egregious fraud and irretrievable injustice. Finding neither, it dismissed the petition, leaving every substantive question — including the propriety of each invocation — to the tribunal yet to be constituted.</p>

<h2>Practical lessons for contractors and employers</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Position after this judgment</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Guarantee already encashed</td><td>Remedy is a money claim in arbitration; Section 9 restitution would be final relief in interim clothing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Guarantee yet to be encashed</td><td>Injunction only on egregious fraud with bank&rsquo;s notice, or genuine irretrievable injustice — commercial hardship does not qualify</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Settlement agreement in the background</td><td>Whether encashment breaches the settlement is itself an arbitrable merits question, not a ground for interim restraint</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Drafting stage</td><td>If the guarantee is unconditional, assume it will be paid on demand; negotiate conditions into the instrument, not into litigation later</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the High Court of Delhi dated 26 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the disputes pending between the parties.</div>
